  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday night through Wednesday)\nIssued at 931 PM CDT Wed Oct 14 2026\n\nHigh pressure at the surface by Sat night should be situated over the \nMidwest and southern Great Lakes. This cold anticyclone bringing a\nN-NE flow across the greater Tennessee Valley will bring the coldest\nair of the season thus far. Night time lows by Sun morning should\nrange from the upper 30s our southern Middle Tennessee counties to \nthe low/mid 40s near large bodies of water. The models have been\nshowing mainly 38 degrees over Lincoln and Moore counties. Under\nclear skies and light winds, we cannot rule out temperatures falling\nanother couple of degrees or so, which would bring a frost risk. This\nis something we will monitor. \n\nThe start of a new week should feature sunny skies with high\ntemperatures a bit warmer than they were Sat, rising into the mid and\nupper 60s. Light winds in the morning should become E-SE in the\nafternoon. Not as cold Sun night with lows in the low/mid 40s. A slow\nwarming trend is expected for the start of the new work week. Under\nmostly sunny to partly cloudy skies, highs Mon should rise into the\nupper 60s to lower 70s, with lows Mon night mid 40s to around 50. The\nmoderation in temperatures continues into the midweek. Under partly\ncloudy skies, highs on Tue should get close to seasonable norms into\nthe low/mid 70s, and into the mid 70s to around 80 Wed. Lows Tue\nnight again should range from the mid 40s to around 50. Dry weather\nshould continue into the middle of next week.",
